      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the product of $24_7 \\times 30_7$ and the sum of its base $7$ digits, we first need to convert these numbers to base $10$ first (for easier multiplication), then convert the result back to base $7$ and finally find the sum of the digits.\n\nStep $1$: Convert $24_7$ and $30_7$ to base $10$.\n\n$24_7 = 2 \\cdot 7^1 + 4 \\cdot 7^0 = 2 \\cdot 7 + 4 \\cdot 1 = 14 + 4 = 18_{10}$.\n\n$30_7 = 3 \\cdot 7^1 + 0 \\cdot 7^0 = 3 \\cdot 7 + 0 \\cdot 1 = 21 + 0 = 21_{10}$.\n\nStep $2$: Multiply them in base $10$.\n\n$18_{10} \\times 21_{10} = 378_{10}$.\n\nStep $3$: Convert the result $378_{10}$ to base $7$.\n\nWe will do this using long division but lengthly.\n\nTo divide $378_{10}$, we find how many times $7^3 = 343_{10}$ goes into $378_{10}$, which leaves a remainder of $378 - 343 = 35_{10}$.\n\nNext, we find how many times $7^2 = 49_{10}$ goes into $35_{10}$, which leaves a remainder of $35 - 49 = -14_{10}$. Since we can't have a negative number in base $7$, we change to negative remainders by borrowing from the next column.\n\nThus, $7^1 = 14_{10}$ goes into $7_{10}$ $\\boxed{1}$ time with a remainder of $3$ (the borrow), making it change to $(-1 + 4)_{10} = 3_{10}$. We borrow $14_{10}$ and continue.\n\nWe get $7_{10}$, $1_{10}$, then we convert $14_{10}$ to $-1 \\cancel{- 1} = -1_{10}$ and $7_{10}$. Adding $(-132)_{10}$ to $24_{10}$ gives us $18_{10}$. Therefore, $18_{10}$ becomes $26_{7}$.\n\nThe final product in base $7$ is $26$, and its sum of digits is $\\boxed{8}$.",
