      "text": "First, let's calculate the total value of \\(24_7\\) in base ten, and then the total value of \\(30_7\\) in base ten. By converting them to base ten, the total value is \\(2 \\times 7 + 4 = 18\\) and \\(3 \\times 7 + 0 = 21\\). To find the product of these two base ten numbers, we multiply \\(21\\) by \\(18\\) in base ten to get \\(378\\).\n\nNext, convert the product, \\(378_{10}\\), to base seven. To do this, we repeatedly divide by 7 and keep track of the remainders. We have:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c c}\n\\text{Dividing by 7} & \\text{Remainder} \\\\\n22 \\div 7 = 3 \\text{ (Remainder 1)} \\\\\n3 \\div 7 = 0 \\text{ (Remainder 3)} \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nReading the remainders from bottom to top gives us the base seven representation:\n\\[\n378_{10} = 313_7\n\\]\nNow, we sum the digits of the base seven number \\(313_7\\). Adding the digits \\(3 + 1 + 3\\):\n\\[\n3 + 1 + 3 = 7\n\\]\nSince \\(7\\) is less than 8, the base seven representation \\(313_7\\) is already its final sum.\n\nHence, the final answer is \\( \\boxed{7} \\).\n\nThis problem examines the conversion between bases and detailed understanding of their arithmetical operations.",
